00:58.000 --> 01:01.000
Hi Adam, I was just listening to
daily source code 367

01:01.000 --> 01:04.000
and you were talking about the
developer APIs

01:04.000 --> 01:06.000
and a source control repository
that you guys were putting up

01:06.000 --> 01:09.000
in order to share it with the
developers.

01:09.000 --> 01:12.000
And this statement kind of
concerned me.

01:12.000 --> 01:18.000
We're setting up a CVS, which is
a revision system.

01:18.000 --> 01:22.000
CVS is a revision system, but
it's a little out of date

01:22.000 --> 01:24.000
and you're going to find a lot
of the maintenance

01:24.000 --> 01:26.000
of the repository to be rather
difficult.

01:26.000 --> 01:30.000
Many large open source software
projects such as KDE,

01:30.000 --> 01:34.000
GCC, Python and Samba are all
moving to Subversion,

01:34.000 --> 01:37.000
which is a revision control
system that was written

01:37.000 --> 01:39.000
specifically to replace CVS.

01:39.000 --> 01:43.000
And it supports a lot of things
that CVS just doesn't support,

01:43.000 --> 01:46.000
like moving things around,
easier authentication,

01:46.000 --> 01:49.000
running your source repository
through Apache.

01:49.000 --> 01:51.000
A lot of people are moving
toward this

01:51.000 --> 01:54.000
revision control system, so I
know I'm geeking out

01:54.000 --> 01:56.000
a little bit here, but I just
wanted to help out

01:56.000 --> 01:59.000
and don't want to see you
actually bring up

01:59.000 --> 02:01.000
all your source code on such an
antiquated system.

02:01.000 --> 02:03.000
I hope this helps. Thanks.

02:03.000 --> 02:05.000
Yeah, thank you very much.

02:05.000 --> 02:07.000
I know exactly what you're
talking about.

02:07.000 --> 02:09.000
I'm not familiar with what
you're talking about.

02:09.000 --> 02:11.000
